[Music]  this morning on the dish  moe's seafood and chowder the  family-owned enterprise has been serving  up bowls of clam chowder and other  seafood treats for  75 years brook silva bragga traveled to  the coastal town of newport oregon  to learn how moe's has been able to  stand the test of taste  and time  long before tourists discovered the  picturesque charm of newport oregon  mahavanimi opened a kind of diner across  from the docks  75 years later mo's seafood and chowder  is an  a institution testament to the power of  a killer recipe  in the family that has kept it cooking  this is her granddaughter  and then we are her great grandchildren  that's cindy dylan and gabrielle mcentee  how old were each of you the first day  you worked in the restaurant i was 12.  for an official job i think i was  probably 12. my first paycheck i was 12  as well  okay so that's the age of maturity at  it's the age of accountability welcome  to 12 get a job  that work ethic was nothing special on  these docks  and actually newport's the number one  dungeness crab producer  in the world the kind of place where  visitors can trap crabs themselves  should i just reach in and grab a few i  wouldn't if i were you but are wise  to leave it to the locals no gloves  no glass no carrie's tough oh my god  handling crap since i was about two  years old  there's a way to do this and there's a  way not to do this grab it from the back  yeah see how it flared out yeah how do  you train people to do this  experience and you can actually taste  that newport sensibility in mo's chowder  we have potatoes clams spices the new  england classic  tailored to the local palette oh yeah  the bacon's right there  yeah yeah these are hardy fishermen and  longshoremen that live here so they want  a food that sticks with them for a while  but settling on that recipe took decades  as cindy saw up close well we had  three main cooks every day the chowder  tasted different no one could follow the  same recipe no they did they did their  own recipe  okay so one it gives you a sense of  moe's management style yeah right  she used to say we're loosely knit  operation you quickly get the impression  that moe's secret ingredient  was always moe herself she was quite a  character  you know sit down with people that she  didn't know and they were like who's  that woman and  and then they realize oh it's moe and  then they're like oh great it's fine if  you smoke  in my face in addition to the chowder  moe's serves up a salt water  encyclopedia  this is a grilled oyster sandwich  including the local catch  and the crab cocktail all paired with  homemade sauces  this is probably the best tartar sauce  in the world and onion rings even better  than that  we make them at home but they don't turn  out like us but none of it compares to  moe's menu of almost too good to believe  origin stories so there's mo right there  okay  like the time bobby kennedy made a  presidential campaign stop  they asked her to go with them this is a  wild story why are they bringing  a chowder person with them on the  campaign i think they just liked her  energy and her  excitement but after a few decades the  excitement of the restaurant  dimmed for mo and she handed the  business to cindy who was just 22.  like it or not i couldn't be a school  teacher if i'd wanted to  this is what i was going to do were you  ever  not that enthusiastic about that kind of  accepted  it was sort of one of those things you  just know that's what you're going to do  it's sort of like being one of the  princes in  england you know you don't have a choice  yeah  queen princess the clam kingdom grew  it now turns out three tons of chowder  every week  there are nine moe's locations but to be  honest by last year the family was  mostly pushing paper a staff of 100 did  the actual cooking  and then last spring happened yeah we  had kind of a little bit of a panic when  we laid off all of our employees  then we had to come up with a plan how  are we going to survive after 74 years  it wasn't clear they'd make it to  another season you would each kind of  been handed  this thing by your grandmother your  great-grandmother  did you worry about what it would mean  if it kind of  died on your watch at first at first i'd  say  march 17th i worried about it  and then when i woke up in the morning  on the 18th  i went i know how to do absolutely  everything in this restaurant  so do my kids let's go do it  for the first time in years mo's recipes  were  actually being made by moe's family so  i'd call my mom and say could you come  down and  teach us how to make cobbler crust and  to sell what they made  dylan drove this trailer 10 000 miles  across the american west  but a lot of it was stuff that we didn't  like to do and it wasn't not fun  it was hard but it was a great way for  the family to like  get glued back together the effort meant  their grandmother  and their great-grandmother and their  great-great-grandmother's little diner  the place that has come to define them  would endure  for a 75th summer we all did it together  so it made it more fulfilling as a  family  for cbs this morning saturday brook  silva brat  newport oregon love everything  about that life yes  we need chowder we need smoke in our  face  from mo right come on that was that was  the line that got  everybody i mean she must have been a  character and i just i just love the  secret recipe  you figure out very quickly is mo right  right and  keeping you know we've seen this in the  pandemic keeping a family together  finding this one silver lining in a  horrible time for so many  they knew how to do it they stuck  together and kept the business in crazy  weird ways bringing them together closer  than ever  gotta love it well

